## Detailed Findings:

### 1. Infrastructure Footprint

- Main domain: `IT company xyz.com`
- Notable subdomains discovered:
  - `vpn.ITcompanyxyz.com`
  - `staging.ITcompanyxyz.com`
  - `dev.ITcompanyxyz.com`
  - `docs.ITcompanyxyz.com` 
- Technology stack: WordPress, AWS, Cloudflare, Microsoft 365
- Certificate Transparency revealed several certificates issued for internal-looking hostnames

### 2. Employee Digital Presence

Eight employees in IT, Operations and Logistics roles were reviewed through public LinkedIn profiles and cross-platform username searches.

Key observations:
- Several employees reuse the same username pattern across GitHub, forums and other services
- Two employees have public GitHub accounts containing references to the company
- Three cases of personal Gmail addresses appearing in professional contexts (document signatures / public files)

### 3. Shadow IT – GitHub Repositories

- User `soandso-dev` (correlated to an employee) maintains a public repository containing VPN configuration files and internal client names
- A second personal repository was found with environment variables and staging credentials references
- Both repositories appear to have been made public unintentionally

### 4. Shadow IT – Cloud Storage

- A publicly accessible bucket named `IT-company-xyz-backups-2024` was identified
- Content includes log files and configuration snippets
- No authentication required at the time of the analysis

### 5. Shadow IT – Collaboration Tools

- An unofficial Notion workspace titled “xyz Clients – Internal” was found via a public link
- Several Telegram groups and one Trello board containing company-related naming were identified
- These tools do not appear in the official technology inventory

---

## Final Hypothesis:

> IT company xyz maintains a relatively standard public infrastructure footprint.  
> However, multiple instances of **Shadow IT** were identified, particularly through personal GitHub repositories and an exposed cloud storage bucket.  
> Employee digital hygiene appears inconsistent, with personal accounts and unofficial collaboration tools being used for work-related activities.  
> The overall risk level is assessed as **Medium**, with specific high-risk findings related to exposed configuration data.

---

## Recommendations:

- [ ] Immediately review and secure the exposed cloud storage bucket
- [ ] Audit personal GitHub/GitLab accounts of technical staff for company-related content
- [ ] Implement a formal Shadow IT discovery and monitoring process
- [ ] Introduce clear policies regarding the use of personal accounts and unofficial tools
- [ ] Conduct periodic Certificate Transparency and subdomain monitoring
- [ ] Provide security awareness training focused on data exposure via public repositories

